# Line endings, and why this file has to exist.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites every text file
# to CRLF on checkout. A shell script with CRLF line endings fails inside a
# Linux container with:
#
#     /usr/local/bin/wd-entrypoint: line 2: $'\r': command not found
#
# or, worse and more often, `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M: no such file
# or directory` -- which reads as a missing interpreter and is a missing
# interpreter, just not the one named. The same applies to anything the gateway
# reads: a Jython timer script with CRLF raises a PySyntaxError on every tick.
#
# So: everything in this repo is LF, on every platform, regardless of what the
# person cloning it has configured. `* text=auto eol=lf` is the whole rule; the
# explicit lines after it exist because the guess is not worth relying on for
# the files that would break loudest.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh      text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.js      text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.css     text eol=lf
Makefile  text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
wd        text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf

# The Windows launcher is the one exception. cmd.exe tolerates LF for simple
# scripts but not reliably for multi-line constructs -- a `for` or an `if (`
# block with LF endings can be parsed a line short, and the error it prints
# names the wrong line. Batch files are CRLF everywhere; keep it that way.
*.cmd     text eol=crlf
*.bat     text eol=crlf

# Binaries: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.modl    binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.gz      binary
*.zip     binary
*.gwbk    binary
*.mp4     binary

# Manifests are parsed by a line regex on two platforms; a CRLF line would
# land a broken entry in a hosts file silently. LF everywhere, enforced.
stacks/*/stack.meta text eol=lf
